\draw[arrow] (data.north) -- (ingest.south);
\draw[arrow] (ingest.north) -- (storage.south);
\draw[dbiarrow] (storage.north) -- (ai.south);
\draw[dbiarrow] (storage.east) .. controls (8,-4.5) and (8,-2.5) .. (llm.east);
\draw[dbiarrow] (ai.north) -- (llm.south);
\draw[arrow] (llm.north) -- (app.south);
\draw[arrow] (ai.east) .. controls (7.5,-3.4) and (7.5,0) .. (app.east);

\end{tikzpicture}%
}
\caption{Six-layer system architecture of the Brand Simulation Engine. Bidirectional
arrows between the Storage, AI, and LLM layers indicate continuous read/write cycles
during simulation execution.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Layer 1 -- Data Sources}

Three classes of data feed the ingestion pipeline: \textbf{Endogenous} inputs (ad
platforms, CRM, promotional data), \textbf{Exogenous} inputs (competitor share of voice
scraped via Firecrawl, macroeconomic time-series), and \textbf{Transactional} inputs
(historical revenue logs, conversion events, LTV).

\subsection{Layer 2 -- Ingestion \& Processing}

Batch data is pulled securely by ETL tools (Airbyte or Fivetran). Real-time scraping
events and streaming telemetry flow through a message queue (Kafka or Redis Pub/Sub),
decoupling ingestion from downstream processing to prevent back-pressure.

\subsection{Layer 3 -- Storage \& Knowledge Base}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Neo4j (Graph DB).}~Serves as the primary relationship mapper, storing
        the marketing input matrix as interconnected nodes and edges
        (e.g., \texttt{Campaign -{[}TARGETS{]}-> AudienceSegment}).
  \item \textbf{Pinecone / Weaviate (Vector Store).}~Stores embeddings of creative assets
        and textual campaign data, enabling semantic similarity search for the GraphRAG
        retrieval pipeline.
  \item \textbf{BigQuery / Snowflake (Data Lake).}~Cold-storage repository for raw,
        unstructured historical logs processed in batch.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Layer 4 -- AI \& Simulation Engine}

Heavy mathematical simulations execute in a dedicated Python environment (FastAPI),
decoupled from the frontend by a Celery task queue backed by Redis:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Macro-Simulation.}~Bayesian MMM implemented via PyMC-Marketing performs
        top-down budget forecasting with Adstock and Hill transformations.
  \item \textbf{Micro-Simulation.}~ABM (powered by Mesa 3.0) and Markov Chains simulate granular user journeys
        and transition probabilities.
  \item \textbf{Optimization Engine.}~NSGA-II Genetic Algorithm (pymoo) evaluates
        thousands of budget combinations to find the Pareto frontier.
  \item \textbf{SHAP Explainability.}~A SHAP TreeExplainer computes the exact
        mathematical contribution of every input feature before passing outputs to the LLM,
        guaranteeing deterministic, hallucination-free explanations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Layer 5 -- LLM Orchestration}

The LLM layer bridges raw simulation outputs and natural-language explainability via the
GraphRAG pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:graphrag}) orchestrated by \textbf{LlamaIndex}. Cloud inference uses Claude~3.5 Sonnet
or Gemini~1.5 Flash via the Vercel AI SDK. An offline fallback deploys \textbf{Gemma4}
locally via Ollama, with a \emph{Fast Profile} that suppresses the internal reasoning
chain for routine explanations and a \emph{Deep Profile} reserved for complex scenario
ideation.

\subsection{Layer 6 -- Application Layer}

Next.js~15 with React Server Components (RSC) renders heavy data server-side, keeping the
client bundle lean. Authentication is managed by Clerk or NextAuth. Visualisations are
built with shadcn/ui, \textbf{Lightweight Charts}, and \textbf{Chart.js}. Full Bangla i18n is implemented via next-intl with
namespaced JSON dictionaries and locale-based sub-path routing.

% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{GraphRAG: Graph-Powered Retrieval-Augmented Generation}
\label{sec:graphrag}

Standard RAG architectures rely on vector similarity search, retrieving text chunks based
on embedding proximity. While effective for simple factual queries, vanilla RAG fails on
the multi-hop reasoning required for marketing simulation \cite{adaptation2026}. A query
such as ``Which campaigns targeting urban millennials overlapped with a competitor's
promotional cycle and achieved a CAC below target?'' requires traversing multiple
relationship hops---impossible for pure vector retrieval.

\textbf{GraphRAG} weaves a structured Neo4j knowledge graph into the retrieval pipeline,
enabling the LLM to reason across relationships and produce grounded, auditable answers.

\subsection{Knowledge Graph Schema}

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption{Neo4j Knowledge Graph Schema}
  \label{tab:graph}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.3}
  \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{>{\bfseries}p{2.8cm} p{5.2cm} X}
    \toprule
    Component & Type & Function \\
    \midrule
    Nodes & Campaign, Channel, Product, AgentCluster, Competitor, Outcome
          & Core entities within the simulation environment \\
    \addlinespace
    Properties & Ad\_Spend, Impressions, Price, Decay\_Rate, Saturation\_Point, Inflation\_Rate
               & Quantitative attributes enabling mathematical transformations \\
    \addlinespace
    Causal Edges & INFLUENCES, SUPPRESSES, CANNIBALIZES, GENERATES
                 & Defines relationships (e.g., Competitor SUPPRESSES Campaign) \\
    \addlinespace
    Structural Edges & ALLOCATED\_TO, TARGETS, BELONGS\_TO, INTERACTS\_WITH
                     & Maps the hierarchy of the marketing mix \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\subsection{Indexing and Community Detection}

The indexing phase transforms unstructured campaign reports and structured transaction
logs into the Neo4j knowledge graph via \textbf{LlamaIndex}. Community
detection algorithms then cluster data hierarchically into semantic groups, and the LLM
pre-generates summaries for each community, enabling both granular local search (entity
neighbourhood traversal) and broad global search (community-level reasoning) without
traversing the entire graph on every query \cite{adaptation2026}.

\subsection{Hybrid Retrieval at Query Time}

A custom retriever combines vector similarity results with k-hop neighbourhood traversal,
passing the synthesised context window to the LLM. A text-to-Cypher translation service
allows the LLM to generate native graph queries dynamically. The resulting answer is not
only factually grounded but carries explicit provenance---the marketer can audit each
recommendation against its source nodes in the graph.

% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Prescriptive Optimisation Engine}
\label{sec:optimisation}

Once the engine has modelled temporal lag, established non-linear saturation curves, and
integrated creative quality coefficients, it must prescribe the mathematically optimal
resource allocation across channels.

\subsection{Genetic Algorithms (NSGA-II)}

The global marketing optimisation problem is a multi-objective, non-linear, heavily
constrained search through a high-dimensional space. Genetic Algorithms simulate natural
selection to iteratively discover optimal solutions \cite{challenge2026}:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*, label=\textbf{Step \arabic*.}]
  \item \textbf{Initialisation.}~A large random population of budget allocations
        (``chromosomes'') is generated across all channels.
  \item \textbf{Evaluation.}~Each allocation is fed into the Bayesian MMM and Hill
        function model to compute its ``fitness'' (projected revenue and ROI).
  \item \textbf{Selection.}~The highest-performing allocations are selected for
        ``reproduction''; sub-optimal ones are discarded.
  \item \textbf{Crossover \& Mutation.}~Successful allocations are mathematically
        combined (crossover), and random variations (mutations) are introduced to
        prevent entrapment in local optima.
\end{enumerate}

Implemented via the \texttt{pymoo} library (NSGA-II algorithm), the engine iterates
thousands of generations in seconds. The output is a \textbf{Pareto frontier}---a
mathematically verified set of budget allocations where no single channel's performance
can be improved without degrading another \cite{adaptation2026}.

\subsection{SHAP Deterministic Explainability}

Before the LLM generates any narrative report or recommendation, all simulation outputs
are passed through a \textbf{SHAP (SHapley Additive Explanations) TreeExplainer}
\cite{blueprint2026}. SHAP computes the exact marginal contribution of every input feature
(budget level, audience segment, platform, competitor index) to the final prediction.
This guarantees that LLM-generated explanations are mathematically anchored to the
model's actual behaviour, eliminating hallucinated justifications.

% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Bangladesh-Specific NLP and Localisation}
\label{sec:bangla}

For the simulation engine to achieve mass adoption among Bangladeshi SME marketers, the
platform must natively understand the linguistic realities of the local market---including
code-mixed ``Banglish'' social media text that standard multilingual models handle poorly
\cite{adaptation2026, blueprint2026}.

\subsection{Bangla-Native NLP Pipeline}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{\texttt{csebuetnlp/banglabert}.}~An ELECTRA-architecture transformer
        trained with a Replaced Token Detection objective, making it exceptionally
        powerful at natively processing noisy, code-mixed Bengali and English social
        media sentiment. Used for sentiment analysis and creative feature extraction from
        Bangla ad copy.
  \item \textbf{\texttt{BAAI/bge-m3}.}~A multi-vector embedding model supporting dense,
        sparse (keyword), and multi-vector retrieval across 100+ languages. It preserves
        the semantic meaning of Banglish queries without collapsing local context, feeding
        clean, culturally aware embeddings into the Pinecone/Weaviate vector store.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Next.js i18n Architecture}

The application implements locale-based sub-path routing
(\texttt{domain.com/bn/dashboard} for Bangla, \texttt{domain.com/en/dashboard} for
English), providing SEO-indexable, locale-specific URLs. Translations are managed with
\textbf{next-intl}, loading namespace-scoped JSON dictionaries (e.g.,
\texttt{bn/finance.json}, \texttt{bn/navigation.json}) exclusively server-side via React
Server Components. TypeScript types enforce compile-time key synchronisation, preventing
silent translation divergence \cite{adaptation2026}.

\subsection{Low-Bandwidth Edge Optimisation}

Rural and peri-urban Bangladesh frequently operates on 2G/3G mobile connections with
high latency and packet loss. The deployment applies four layers of mitigation:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*, label=(\arabic*)]
  \item \textbf{Font Subsetting.}~The \texttt{next/font} module self-hosts fonts and
        generates subset files containing only the Unicode ranges required for Bangla,
        reducing font payloads from $\sim$600\,KB to under 50\,KB.
  \item \textbf{Incremental Static Regeneration (ISR).}~Standard reporting views and
        historical Pareto frontiers are pre-computed and cached on Vercel's global Edge
        Network, serving requests from geographically proximate nodes in under
        100\,milliseconds.
  \item \textbf{Selective Prefetching.}~The default aggressive Next.js \texttt{<Link>}
        prefetching is disabled for non-critical routes. Prefetch triggers are bound to
        explicit user intent (hover / touch), preventing wasteful background data
        transfers on metered mobile connections.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic Imports.}~Heavy visualisation libraries such as Lightweight Charts and Chart.js are
        loaded via \texttt{next/\allowbreak dynamic} with lazy evaluation, minimising
        the initial Time to Interactive (TTI) on low-end devices.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Offline / Rural Fallback Mode}

For users with intermittent connectivity, the architecture provides an offline-capable
local LLM profile. \textbf{Gemma4} is deployed via \textbf{Ollama} on the user's
device. A \emph{Fast Profile} configures the model for concise, low-latency
explanations on resource-constrained hardware. A \emph{Deep Profile} enables
full extended-thinking reasoning for complex scenario ideation when resources
permit \cite{blueprint2026}.

% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Executive Dashboard and Decision-Support Metrics}
\label{sec:dashboard}

The complex mathematics of Bayesian inference, Markov Chains, and Genetic Algorithms
must be abstracted into an intuitive, forward-looking interface for marketing executives
and CFOs \cite{challenge2026, adaptation2026}.

\subsection{Component Architecture}

The dashboard is built on \textbf{Next.js~15 RSC} and \textbf{shadcn/ui}---a collection
of re-usable components copied directly into source code (rather than installed as an
opaque NPM dependency), granting full ownership and deep customisation. A persistent
sidebar transitions to a Sheet drawer on mobile. TanStack Table powers sortable,
filterable \texttt{DataTable} components handling tens of thousands of transactional rows
without degrading performance.

